WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
How can binding outside a cell load a molecular switch inside it?

2
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.180
Follow one membrane as the message crosses without the ligand crossing.

3
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:23.033
A G-protein-coupled receptor, or GPCR, is one chain woven repeatedly through the

4
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:27.893
membrane. Its outer pocket and inner face are physically connected.

5
00:00:29.193 --> 00:00:32.913
An agonist is a ligand that favors receptor activation.

6
00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:37.453
It settles into the pocket and favors an active shape.

7
00:00:37.453 --> 00:00:42.573
One cytoplasmic helix swings outward, opening a cavity below.

8
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:47.317
A membrane-bound G protein waits below as a trimer.

9
00:00:48.577 --> 00:00:50.537
Purple Gα alpha holds gray GDP.

10
00:00:52.157 --> 00:00:54.197
Teal βγ stays pressed against it.

11
00:00:55.357 --> 00:00:57.717
In this resting arrangement, the switch is off.

12
00:00:58.977 --> 00:01:04.217
The Gα alpha tail rises into the receptor cavity, bringing the whole trimer to the

13
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:05.217
active receptor.

14
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:09.207
Docking changes Gα's grip on GDP.

15
00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:12.427
The gray nucleotide leaves first.

16
00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:16.607
Green GTP can then occupy the empty site.

17
00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:22.427
The receptor has acted as an exchange factor, not as a source of GTP.

18
00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:28.167
It remains ready to load another G protein while agonist stays bound.

19
00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:34.887
GTP changes the Gα -alpha surface and frees βγ to engage targets.

20
00:01:36.187 --> 00:01:38.127
The textbook picture draws them apart.

21
00:01:39.647 --> 00:01:43.387
Activated Gi may instead rearrange without fully separating.

22
00:01:44.787 --> 00:01:47.687
Either way, both surfaces can regulate effectors.

23
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:53.687
Hydrolysis later returns Gα -alpha to GDP and favors reassembly.

24
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.490
Now the cell chooses what those surfaces meet.

25
00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.110
Follow the hourglass.

26
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:06.750
Many receptors narrow to a few Gα -alpha families and βγ, then widen toward enzymes

27
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.890
and channels. One branch raises cAMP.

28
00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:13.330
Another lowers it.

29
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:15.910
Another raises calcium.

30
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.450
βγ can open a potassium channel.

31
00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:25.693
Each family points to a main direct target, not a guaranteed exclusive partner.

32
00:02:26.893 --> 00:02:28.813
The receptor makes the first selection.

33
00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:34.473
The cell then supplies whichever enzymes, channels, and scaffolds are present.

34
00:02:35.753 --> 00:02:39.213
So one messenger can carry different meanings in different cells.

35
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:43.957
The same acetylcholine can therefore meet different wiring.

36
00:02:45.237 --> 00:02:51.577
In a pacemaker cell, the receptor on screen loads Gi, and freed βγ opens potassium

37
00:02:51.577 --> 00:02:54.217
channels. Firing slows.

38
00:02:55.717 --> 00:03:01.197
In airway muscle, a different receptor loads Gq, and phospholipase C-β -beta raises

39
00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:04.037
calcium. The muscle contracts.

40
00:03:05.237 --> 00:03:07.917
The input matches; the cellular paths differ.

41
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.970
So do not predict an answer from the ligand alone.

42
00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.850
Trace the receptor subtype, the G protein it loads, and the targets available.

43
00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.450
That path predicts the cell's first response.

44
00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.010
Dose and efficacy still change how strongly the receptor is activated.

45
00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.110
The wiring decides what that activation means.
